C = [
("h1", "10. Galatians 5:1–15: Liberty Called, Liberty Guarded"),
("p", "Claim: Galatians 5:1–15 speaks of liberty on two fronts. Against those who would put the believers back under a yoke, Paul commands them to stand fast. Against the misuse of liberty, he commands them not to use it for the flesh but to serve one another by love. The passage is not about meats, and it does not take up the question of the weak brother. It marks the edges of the liberty the other passages assume."),
("sub", "10.1 Stand fast"),
("p", "“Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free, and be not entangled again with the yoke of bondage” (Galatians 5:1). The command has two halves, one to hold and one to refuse. The liberty is a liberty “wherewith Christ hath made us free,” so it is Christ’s gift, and the yoke it contrasts with is “the yoke of bondage.” The letter is written “unto the churches of Galatia” (Galatians 1:2), and it was occasioned by a push to circumcise them. Paul states the consequence in the next verses. “Behold, I Paul say unto you, that if ye be circumcised, Christ shall profit you nothing. For I testify again to every man that is circumcised, that he is a debtor to do the whole law. Christ is become of no effect unto you, whosoever of you are justified by the law; ye are fallen from grace” (Galatians 5:2–4). The volume reads these verses as written and takes from them only what bears on liberty: the Galatians were being drawn to a requirement Paul says they must not accept. Circumcision is read in Study 51, the Jerusalem Council in Study 19, and the Law’s jurisdiction in Study 59."),
("sub", "10.2 Faith that worketh by love"),
("p", "“For we through the Spirit wait for the hope of righteousness by faith. For in Jesus Christ neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision; but faith which worketh by love” (Galatians 5:5, 6). The sentence has the form of Romans 14:17 and 1 Corinthians 8:8. It sets a thing in its place by saying what it cannot do: circumcision and uncircumcision do not avail. What avails is “faith which worketh by love.” The sentence does not say that the Galatians may be circumcised if they wish. The surrounding verses say they must not. The negation is of the thing’s power, and it does not turn the thing into an open question."),
("p", "The volume marks the difference between this sentence and the sentences about meats. In Romans 14 Paul says that meats and days are left to conscience and that each party is to receive the other. In Galatians 5 he says that circumcision, taken as a requirement, makes Christ of no effect. The two are not the same kind of matter. A matter is open when Paul leaves it open. Circumcision as the Galatians were being asked to accept it is not left open. The volume does not turn this into a general principle about every ritual matter. It reports the two cases as the letters give them."),
("sub", "10.3 The troubler and the leaven"),
("p", "“Ye did run well; who did hinder you that ye should not obey the truth? This persuasion cometh not of him that calleth you. A little leaven leaveneth the whole lump” (Galatians 5:7–9). “I have confidence in you through the Lord, that ye will be none otherwise minded: but he that troubleth you shall bear his judgment, whosoever he be” (Galatians 5:10). Paul speaks of the persuasion, not of the persons first, and then of the troubler: “I would they were even cut off which trouble you” (Galatians 5:12). The words are sharp, and they are about those who were putting the yoke on the Galatians. They are not about weak brothers. The volume notes the contrast with Romans 14, where Paul has no such words for the weak man. The brother who abstains from meat is received. The troubler who puts a yoke on believers is judged. The difference lies in what each does to the others."),
("sub", "10.4 Called unto liberty, not for the flesh"),
("p", "“For, brethren, ye have been called unto liberty; only use not liberty for an occasion to the flesh, but by love serve one another” (Galatians 5:13). Here is the guard. The liberty is real: “ye have been called unto liberty.” The limit is also real: “only use not liberty for an occasion to the flesh.” Constable remarks that Paul warns “against using their liberty as a license to sin to prevent them from overreacting,” and the volume agrees with the observation. The liberty from the yoke is not liberty to sin. The positive end is named at once: “but by love serve one another.” The believers are free from the yoke so that they may serve."),
("p", "“For all the law is fulfilled in one word, even in this; Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself” (Galatians 5:14). The verse says that the law is fulfilled in love. It does not say that the believer is under the law, and Paul has just said he is not to take the yoke. Constable puts it, “Under grace we are free to fulfill the Law by loving one another.” The volume reads the verse as written. Whether the Body stands under the law in any sense is the question of Study 59, and the volume says only what the verse says: the law is fulfilled in love, and love is what the liberty is for."),
("p", "“But if ye bite and devour one another, take heed that ye be not consumed one of another” (Galatians 5:15). The verse shows what the misuse looks like: not a vice in the list of verses 19 to 21, but the quarrel. The limit of liberty is partly a limit on the Galatians’ treatment of each other, and the quarrel in Romans 14 between the eater and the abstainer is the same kind of danger in a different place."),
("ctbl", "Galatians 5:1–15 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "Yes: stand fast; use not liberty for the flesh; serve one another by love", "5:1, 13"),
    ("What limit?", "Do not be entangled again in the yoke; do not use liberty as an occasion to the flesh", "5:1, 13"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To Christ, who made them free, and to one another", "5:1, 13"),
    ("What ground?", "Christ hath made us free; ye have been called unto liberty; faith worketh by love", "5:1, 6, 13"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "Whether the Body is under the law in any sense; what “the yoke” includes beyond circumcision", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "10.5 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Liberty is Christ’s gift, and Paul commands the Galatians to hold it against a yoke.",
    "The negation “neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision” sets the thing in its place and does not make circumcision an open question for the Galatians.",
    "Liberty is not to be used for an occasion to the flesh, and its end is to serve one another by love.",
    "The jurisdiction of the law is left to Study 59, circumcision to Study 51 and the Jerusalem Council to Study 19.",
  ]),
]
